## What is GraphQL?

GraphQL is a **query language for APIs and a server-side runtime** created by Meta in 2012. Instead of calling multiple fixed REST endpoints (`/users/1`, `/users/1/posts`, `/users/1/followers`), GraphQL provides a **single endpoint** (`POST /graphql`) where clients request the exact fields they need in a single round-trip.

## The Core Operations

### 1. Query (Read Data)
```graphql
query GetUserProfile($userId: ID!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    id
    username
    avatarUrl
    badges(limit: 3) {
      name
      iconUrl
    }
    stats {
      rank
      totalXp
    }
  }
}
```

### 2. Mutation (Write Data)
```graphql
mutation CompleteQuest($questId: ID!, $score: Int!) {
  submitQuestResult(questId: $questId, score: $score) {
    success
    xpAwarded
    newLevel
  }
}
```

### 3. Subscription (Real-Time Streams)
```graphql
subscription OnLeaderboardUpdate {
  leaderboardTopChanged {
    rank
    username
    totalXp
  }
}
```

## Schema & Resolvers

A GraphQL server defines a strongly-typed schema (SDL):

```graphql
type Player {
  id: ID!
  username: String!
  level: Int!
  inventory: [Item!]!
}

type Query {
  player(id: ID!): Player
}
```

Each field is backed by a **resolver function** that executes database or service calls:

```javascript
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    player: async (_, { id }, context) => context.db.player.findUnique({ where: { id } }),
  },
  Player: {
    inventory: async (parent, _, context) => context.db.items.findMany({ where: { playerId: parent.id } }),
  }
};
```

## The N+1 Query Problem & DataLoader

When fetching nested relations (e.g., 20 users with their team details), naive resolvers run 1 query for users + 20 queries for teams (21 total queries). **DataLoader** solves this by batching and caching ID lookups into a single SQL `WHERE id IN (...)` query.

## What are Webhooks?

Webhooks are **user-defined HTTP callbacks (reverse APIs)**. Instead of your client polling a server repeatedly to check if an event happened, the provider **pushes an HTTP POST** payload to your server the instant the event occurs.

## Polling vs Webhooks

```
POLLING (Pull model):
Every 10s: GET /orders/new → "no new orders" (99.9% wasted calls)

WEBHOOKS (Push model):
Order placed → POST /webhooks/orders { "event": "order.paid" }
Zero wasted requests, sub-second notification latency.
```

## HMAC Signature Verification

Because webhook endpoints are public, anyone can send fake requests. Providers include a cryptographic signature in headers:

```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');

function verifyWebhook(rawBody, signatureHeader, webhookSecret) {
  const [timestampPart, sigPart] = signatureHeader.split(',');
  const timestamp = timestampPart.split('=')[1];
  const signature = sigPart.split('=')[1];

  // Prevent replay attacks (reject payloads older than 5 minutes)
  if (Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - Number(timestamp)) > 300) {
    throw new Error('Webhook timestamp expired');
  }

  const expectedSig = crypto
    .createHmac('sha256', webhookSecret)
    .update(`${timestamp}.${rawBody}`)
    .digest('hex');

  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(signature), Buffer.from(expectedSig));
}
```

## Idempotency & Delivery Guarantees

Webhooks guarantee **at-least-once delivery**. Due to network retries, your webhook receiver may receive duplicate events. Store event IDs in your database and discard duplicates before processing.
